enoble there were a lot of swedish  girls down there so there's a real  incentive so i spent a fair amount of  time with them so that was very good for  my swedish  um not probably so good for my french  but it was good for my swedish  um  so the whole the swedish thing started  the kind of and i'm sure that that  having heard it for five years as a  child it was you know it helped me  uh but i had a very limited vocabulary  then uh after starting my own company in  1987  we ended up doing business in sweden  uh because we wanted to  uh basically develop you know canadian  lumber spruce and pine we wanted to  grade it to the european specification  so  um  i went to sweden  and found we found a guy who was a  retired mill manager who was willing to  come out  and teach us the canadian mills how to  grade this lumber to european  specifications so i started having this  involvement with sweden  and lo and behold in  1993 there was a dramatic shift in world  lumber markets whereby  we could no longer export from north  america because the prices in north  america were so high  but we could buy in sweden and sell to  japan  so i started doing more and more  business in sweden so then i decided to  really work on improving my swedish not  that i needed to because  the people we dealt with all spoke  english with a few exceptions  but i wanted to really improve my  swedish and basically what i did was  listen and read and the biggest  influence on my swedish by far  was this  herman lindqvist historian omisvaria  history  of sweden okay and  i mean this he writes the most wonderful  popularization of history now it just so  happens that i like history and of  course it's very important that you read  about things that you like  and not only  did he have this book but he also read  his book  so i was able to listen to the book and  read the book and i have listened to  herman lindquist  so much and so many times  and this one is kind of like a summary  uh no this is one of the sections  drummer overclick which is i think the  modern period but he also has one where  he talks about the history of sweden  from the ice age till the future or till  the frontier i guess i don't know what  that is i don't know my vocabulary in  sweden sometimes is lacking the present  day i guess historian um from east t dil  franti  so there you got all this good stuff to  listen to i also got very interested i'd  go to sweden and i just go and buy all  the audiobooks i could find there was an  excellent series on how to make speeches  in swedish and i don't know how many  times i listen to this  and of course everyone then because  there's another one on  charles the 12th  there's one on gustavosa  they even did a thing on napoleon and i  mean these are just some i mean  well done  audiobooks  are so wonderful and i can still  remember when i'm in sweden and i fly  out there in the winter and it's you  know i can't sleep because of the jet  lag i'm up at four o'clock i go jogging  in the snow  listening to  you know  herman lindquist over and over again and  reading and of course when i'm there i  read the newspaper  and um  yeah that was uh that was it now i have  great gaps in my vocabulary in swedish i  would like to at some point really work  on my swedish uh here at link and all  saving the words and working the words  uh but i mean i can work my way through  a history book  uh history books are for me easy to read  because i i i just i'm so interested  that even like in czech as i've  mentioned i read my check history book  i can understand what they're talking  about and so eventually you know you  start to get used to the vocabulary it's  not ideal  more ideal is is i guess what we've done  at link but uh yeah that's basically  swedish uh  undoubtedly helped by the fact that i  grew up i spent the first five years of  my life speaking sweetie but then i  completely forgot it  and now i have an adult vocabulary in  swedish even though there are gaps in it  that i would like to work at so there's  not  much more to say on swedish i ended up  having quite a uh an involvement with  sweden in the timber trade because we  for we still export lumber from sweden  to japan and i would go there two three  times a year and and even though  a lot of the people there speak english  the business people and so forth uh very  often they're happy to speak in their  own language if you speak someone's  language well  people are happy to speak their own  language i often hear this complaint  that people won't speak to me in english  and stuff and there are people like that  but  most people are  most comfortable speaking their own  language and that's certainly what i  found in sweden and i love going there i  like having the act of eat in fact we  had aquavit tonight because uh we often  before dinner  my wife always makes a little hors  d'oeuvre  and tonight  we will take some salmon  and slice it up for sashimi  and i like to pour olive oil  on my uh sashimi  and with a little wasabi of course and  soy sauce and because it's raw  just like in sweden when you have like  gravadlox which is their  cured  you know salmon you have it with aqua  meat so we had a little glass of  activity before we had our meal  uh the main meal was chicken and with  lots of greens and stuff to which we had  a little bit of wine so we had a very  nice meal  but i always have that you know  attachment to many things in sweden  including of course the aquavit the  gravadlocks  and the history the language and all  these things like with every language  that you learn  you bring so much with it so many things  that you can enjoy so there you have it  that's sweet
 

